# Which line is longer?
subtitle: "Two lines, one looks longer: tap the longer one, then see why the Müller-Lyer illusion fools almost everyone, even when you know the trick."

## Which line is longer?
affirm: playful
sources:
    - id: ml
      title: Optische Urteilstäuschungen
      authors: F. C. Müller-Lyer
      year: 1889
      finding: 'the original illusion: fins pointing out make a line look longer than fins pointing in'
    - id: amir
      title: Is visual perception WEIRD? The Müller-Lyer illusion and the Cultural Byproduct Hypothesis
      authors: D. Amir, C. Firestone et al.
      year: 2025
      finding: the 1960s reports that some cultures barely see the illusion rest on flawed methods; the illusion appears to be universal

    path ln_a points [[230,585],[770,585]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    group top_fins members ["tf1","tf2","tf3","tf4"] hidden true
    path tf1 points [[230,585],[168,653]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    path tf2 points [[230,585],[168,517]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    path tf3 points [[770,585],[832,653]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    path tf4 points [[770,585],[832,517]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    path ln_b points [[230,335],[770,335]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    group bottom_fins members ["bf1","bf2","bf3","bf4"] hidden true
    path bf1 points [[230,335],[292,403]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    path bf2 points [[230,335],[292,267]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    path bf3 points [[770,335],[708,403]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    path bf4 points [[770,335],[708,267]] style.stroke_width 7 hidden true

    card title "Which line is longer?"

Here is a line with its fins flared out, like the feathers on an arrow's tail.
    group top_line members ["ln_a","top_fins"]

And here is a second one, with its ends folded in, like arrowheads.
    group bottom_line members ["ln_b","bottom_fins"]

    box same "Same length" x 500 y 200 width 260 font_size 38

    ask "Which line is longer? Tap it, or tap Same length." choose ["top_line","bottom_line","same"] answer same store c
        right: They are exactly the same length.
        wrong: Look again in a moment.

    erase same

Drop a straight edge from each end, and both lines start and stop at the same place.
    path g1 dashed #b5482e points [[230,655],[230,240]] style.stroke_width 2
    path g2 dashed #b5482e points [[770,655],[770,240]] style.stroke_width 2
    parallel true

Take the fins away, and the two lines are plainly twins.
    erase top_fins bottom_fins

Put them back, and the top one looks longer again, even though you now know better.
    draw top_fins bottom_fins parallel true

This is the Müller-Lyer illusion, drawn by the German Franz Müller-Lyer in 1889.
    highlight top_line bottom_line

In the 1960s, researchers reported that people who grew up without square rooms, such as San foragers in the Kalahari, hardly saw it.
    highlight top_fins

But a 2025 review found those studies had serious flaws. The illusion seems to come with our eyes and brains, not with our buildings.
    highlight bottom_fins
---
